From fe4927110fa8ef0f11ecf74e339ee40d0a182fd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 11:51:59 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 03/14] qualcommax: ipq807x: add support for Arista C-360

Arista C-360 is a Wi-Fi 6E enterprise access point with four radios and
two 10 GbE ports that both take PoE.

Hardware specification:
- SoC: Qualcomm IPQ8076A (4x Cortex-A53, up to 2.2 GHz)
- RAM: 2 GiB DDR4
- Flash: 32 MiB SPI NOR (Macronix MX25U25635F), 1 GiB parallel NAND
- Ethernet: 2x 10GBASE-T (Aquantia AQR113C over USXGMII), PoE in on both
- Wi-Fi:
  - IPQ8074: 2.4 GHz 4x4 and 5 GHz 4x4 802.11ax
  - QCN9074 (PCIe 0): 6 GHz 4x4 802.11ax
  - QCN9072 (PCIe 1): 2.4/5/6 GHz 2x2 802.11ax (the QCN9074's 2x2
    sibling, same 17cb:1104)
- Bluetooth LE: Nordic nRF52833 (HCI over UART)
- TPM: Infineon SLB 9670 (TPM 2.0, SPI)
- USB: 1x USB 3.0 Type-A
- LEDs: power (green, orange), Ethernet 1 and 2 (green, orange),
  2.4 GHz, 5 GHz, 6 GHz (green), on two 74LV164 shift registers
- Button: reset
- UART: RJ-45 console port, RS-232 levels, 115200 8N1

MAC addresses:
- Ethernet 1 (lan1): 0:art 0x0 (label, 30:86:2d:xx:xx:xx)
- Ethernet 2 (lan2): 0:art 0x6 (label - 1)
- Wi-Fi: from the calibration data (label - 63, - 47, - 31 and - 15)

Calibration data for all three Wi-Fi chips is read from 0:art through
NVMEM cells (0x1000, 0x26800 and 0x4c000).

Ethernet 1 alone is in the LAN by default, so that both ports cabled to
one switch (as for PoE redundancy) make no loop; add lan2 to br-lan, or
bond the two, as needed.

Flash layout: the stock firmware keeps two NAND slots of 384 MiB,
"rootfs" (slot 0) and "rootfs_1" (slot 1), their kernels in SPI NOR
(0:hlos, 0:hlos_1). OpenWrt takes slot 0 for UBI (kernel FIT, rootfs,
rootfs_data); slot 1 stays as a fallback. The device tree appends
ubi.mtd=rootfs and root=/dev/ubiblock0_1, so the root is found whatever
U-Boot passes.

Flash instructions (console needed):
1. Stop U-Boot: it waits 2 s and stops only for the string
   s6.D5r4Gnb89ZEWSW6Kq.
   Do not let the stock firmware boot meanwhile: its boot script saves the
   U-Boot environment on every boot.
2. Boot the initramfs from a TFTP server (nothing is saved):
     setenv ipaddr <AP address>; setenv serverip <TFTP server>
     setenv fdt_high 0x4A3E0000
     setenv bootargs console=ttyMSM0,115200n8 rootwait panic=0
     tftpboot 0xA0000000 openwrt-qualcommax-ipq807x-arista_c-360-initramfs-uImage.itb
     bootm 0xA0000000#config@hk09
   fdt_high is needed: the stock value places the device tree on top of
   U-Boot's own. panic=0 halts a failed boot instead of rebooting into the
   stock firmware, which would save the environment.
3. Back up all MTD partitions; 0:art holds the unit's calibration data and
   MAC addresses and exists nowhere else.
4. Copy the factory image to /tmp and write it to slot 0 ("rootfs" in
   /proc/mtd):
     ubidetach -p /dev/mtdN; ubiformat /dev/mtdN -y -f /tmp/factory.ubi
5. Set the boot command, in one environment write, and reboot:
     fw_setenv -s - <<'EOF'
     owrt_args console=ttyMSM0,115200n8 rootwait panic=10
     owrt_parts setenv mtdids nand0=nand0; setenv mtdparts mtdparts=nand0:0x18000000@0x0(ubi)
     owrt_load ubi part ubi && ubi read 0x44000000 kernel
     owrt_boot setenv fdt_high 0x4A3E0000; run owrt_parts owrt_load && bootm 0x44000000#config@hk09
     arista_unset setenv mtdids; setenv mtdparts; setenv fdt_high 0x4A400000
     arista_args if test ${pri} = 0; then setenv bootargs ${ba0}; else setenv bootargs ${ba1}; fi
     arista_boot run arista_unset arista_args ubootscript
     bootcmd setenv bootargs ${owrt_args}; run owrt_boot; run arista_boot
     pri 1
     EOF

The stock boot (bootipq) checks image signatures; this boot command loads
OpenWrt's kernel from UBI with bootm instead and falls back to the stock
boot, slot 1. The stock boot script sets bootargs only when the slot
changes and then saves the environment, so the fallback first puts the
stock bootargs, fdt_high and partition settings back. Later upgrades are
ordinary sysupgrades.

Return to stock: fw_setenv bootcmd 'run ubootscript', then reboot; the
stock firmware in slot 1 boots.

Known limitations:
- The QCN9074 and QCN9072 radios start only with Arista's own build of the
  firmware (WLAN.HK.2.5.r4). With every public ath11k-firmware build
  tested (2.5.0.1 to 2.9.0.1), the firmware stops answering at the QMI
  capability request. A following commit copies Arista's build from the
  unit's own Arista system.
- Bluetooth LE is attached by a following commit (bluetooth-uart).
- The SoC's second USB controller is left disabled, as in Arista's own
  device tree: enabled, its PHY doesn't lock.
- Only the vendor's 6 GHz radio plan is supported. gpio9 (exported as
  "dual-5g", low) selects the plan; the vendor's dual 5 GHz plan also
  needs other calibration (ART 0x71800) and board data for the IPQ8074
  and the PCIe 0 card.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Christopher Griffin <christophergriffin05@gmail.com>
